她依然保留了她那标志性的秘诀，在摔倒的相反方向

636
00:24:04.190 --> 00:24:06.270
as you fall in the opposite direction.
稍微看一眼，然后身体往反方向倒去。

637
00:24:06.270 --> 00:24:07.630
And it's just so funny.
这真是太搞笑了。

638
00:24:07.630 --> 00:24:08.590
She's just great at that.
她在这方面真的很棒。

639
00:24:08.590 --> 00:24:12.750
It's so funny because that's like a magician, like it's a misdirection.
太搞笑了，因为这就跟魔术师一样，就像是一种误导。

640
00:24:12.750 --> 00:24:15.230
It's a little misdirect, like a little, yeah, a little prestige
这就叫小误导，就像变魔术时的小戏法

641
00:24:15.230 --> 00:24:16.670
or like a little something over here.
或者像是在这边搞点小动作。

642
00:24:16.670 --> 00:24:17.950
And then you go the other way with it.
然后你突然转向另一边。

643
00:24:17.950 --> 00:24:20.190
And even I said to like Rebecca and I were watching it,
甚至我对丽贝卡说，我们当时在看的时候，

644
00:24:20.190 --> 00:24:21.950
I hit pause and I said, let's see.
我按了暂停说，咱们瞧瞧。

645
00:24:21.950 --> 00:24:23.710
I said, I know it's going to be a seating fall.
我说，我知道这会是一个坐姿摔倒。

646
00:24:23.710 --> 00:24:27.310
Let's see if Sarah does a little look the other way before she falls,
咱们看看莎拉在摔倒前是不是又往反方向看了一眼，

647
00:24:27.310 --> 00:24:29.390
because she said to, she explained to Josh,
因为她曾对乔什解释过，

648
00:24:29.390 --> 00:24:30.590
that's part of her secret.
那是她秘诀的一部分。

649
00:24:30.590 --> 00:24:31.790
And we were like, she got it.
然后我们当时就说，她成功了。

650
00:24:31.790 --> 00:24:34.190
She just looks the other way right when she's going to take the fall,
她就在准备摔倒的那一刻，往相反的方向看了一眼，

651
00:24:34.190 --> 00:24:35.790
just even in a seated fall.
哪怕只是坐在椅子上摔倒。

652
00:24:35.790 --> 00:24:37.390
She's technically excellent.
她的技术极其精湛。

653
00:24:37.390 --> 00:24:37.950
You know, it's funny.
你知道的，这挺有意思的。

654
00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:41.950
I think there's this assumption that artists are always on some level,
我觉得大家有一种假设，认为艺术家在某种程度上

655
00:24:41.950 --> 00:24:44.990
like just intuitive and dope.
就像是纯粹靠直觉而且天赋异禀。

656
00:24:44.990 --> 00:24:48.270
But if you talk to like people who are great artists,
但如果你和那些伟大的艺术家交流过，

657
00:24:48.350 --> 00:24:50.590
they're like, no, I know exactly what I'm doing.
他们会说，不，我完全知道自己在做什么。

658
00:24:50.590 --> 00:24:52.590
I know how to make this work.
我知道该怎么让这个奏效。

659
00:24:53.310 --> 00:24:55.550
Did you read, I don't know if we've talked about this book.
你读过吗，我不知道我们有没有聊过这本书。

660
00:24:57.790 --> 00:24:59.950
Meet Me in the Bathroom by Lizzie Goodman.
莉兹·古德曼写的《在浴室相遇》。

661
00:24:59.950 --> 00:25:01.870
No, it's an oral history.
没有，这是一本口述历史。

662
00:25:01.870 --> 00:25:03.790
Did you read, please kill me the oral history of cooking?
你读过吗，库克的口述历史《请杀死我》？

663
00:25:03.790 --> 00:25:04.510
Please kill me.
《请杀死我》。

664
00:25:04.510 --> 00:25:05.310
I've read it more than once.
我读不止一遍了。

665
00:25:05.310 --> 00:25:06.270
If you love, please kill me.
如果你喜欢《请杀死我》。

666
00:25:06.270 --> 00:25:08.350
I'll send you this book because I love this book so much.
我会把这本书寄给你，因为我太喜欢它了。

667
00:25:09.150 --> 00:25:11.470
And I know Lizzie a bit and she's wonderful.
我和莉兹有点交情，她人很好。

668
00:25:11.470 --> 00:25:16.430
And this book is an oral history of early 2000s,
这本书是关于2000年代初期的口述历史，

669
00:25:16.430 --> 00:25:18.190
kind of indie rock New York City based.
大致是立足于纽约市的独立摇滚。

670
00:25:18.190 --> 00:25:19.310
So it starts with the strokes.
所以它是从The Strokes（洞穴乐队/号角乐队）开始的。

671
00:25:19.310 --> 00:25:20.830
I think you've mentioned this to me before.
我想你以前跟我提过这个。

672
00:25:20.830 --> 00:25:22.910
Yeah, it goes through like TV on the radio.
对，它一直讲到像TV on the Radio这样的乐队。

673
00:25:22.910 --> 00:25:23.310
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
对，对，对。

674
00:25:23.310 --> 00:25:27.150
It's up through basically Vampire Weekend 2011.
它基本上一直讲到2011年的Vampire Weekend（吸血鬼周末）。

675
00:25:27.150 --> 00:25:27.790
Yeah.
对。

676
00:25:27.790 --> 00:25:32.350
So there's this great bit where Matt Barringer,
所以这里有个很棒的片段，马特·巴林格——

677
00:25:32.350 --> 00:25:33.710
is he from the national, is that his name?
他是The National（国家乐队）的吗，他叫这个名字吗？

678
00:25:33.710 --> 00:25:34.590
Matt Barringer?
马特·巴林格？

679
00:25:34.590 --> 00:25:35.150
Yeah.
对。

680
00:25:35.150 --> 00:25:35.470
Yeah.
对。

681
00:25:35.470 --> 00:25:39.390
So he says being a rock star is the stupidest,
所以他说，当摇滚巨星是最愚蠢、

682
00:25:39.390 --> 00:25:41.630
most embarrassing thing you could ever be.
最尴尬的事情。

683
00:25:42.590 --> 00:25:47.310
He said, I had to create a persona for myself that was a rock star.
他说，我必须为自己创造一个人设，那就是摇滚巨星。

684
00:25:47.950 --> 00:25:48.110
Yeah.
对。

685
00:25:48.110 --> 00:25:48.990
That was not me.
那不是我。

686
00:25:49.470 --> 00:25:50.110
Right.
没错。

687
00:25:50.110 --> 00:25:54.110
So he's like, he's got this role he plays when he's on stage
所以他说，他在台上时会扮演这样一个角色，

688
00:25:54.110 --> 00:25:55.870
because he's like, it's humiliating otherwise.
因为他觉得不然的话太丢脸了。

689
00:25:55.870 --> 00:25:59.710
Like I have to create this kind of shell or this kind of armor
就像我必须为自己创造一种外壳或某种盔甲，

690
00:25:59.710 --> 00:26:01.470
for myself that's like another character.
就像另一个角色一样。

691
00:26:01.470 --> 00:26:03.230
This isn't totally, and I like what we're talking about,
这不完全是，而且我喜欢我们谈论的内容，

692
00:26:03.230 --> 00:26:06.670
but I do think that when you interrogate great artists
但我确实觉得，当你盘问伟大的艺术家

693
00:26:06.670 --> 00:26:08.350
about like, what are you doing?
关于比如“你在干什么？”时，

694
00:26:08.350 --> 00:26:13.390
You know, if you ask like Iggy Pop or David Bowie or Stevie Nix
你知道的，如果你去问像伊基·波普、大卫·鲍伊、斯蒂薇·尼克斯

695
00:26:13.390 --> 00:26:15.870
or people that have a kind of person.
或者那些具有某种人设的人。

696
00:26:15.870 --> 00:26:15.950
Oh yeah.
哦，对。

697
00:26:15.950 --> 00:26:17.310
They know the construct.
他们很清楚这个架构。

698
00:26:17.310 --> 00:26:18.590
They know what they're doing.
他们知道自己在做什么。

699
00:26:18.590 --> 00:26:22.670
Like Stevie Nix shows every article of clothing
就像斯蒂薇·尼克斯展示每一件衣服

700
00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:27.550
to create the persona and further the persona.
来打造人设并强化人设一样。

701
00:26:27.550 --> 00:26:30.830
And I think great artists like really know what they're doing.
而且我认为伟大的艺术家其实很清楚他们在做什么。

702
00:26:30.830 --> 00:26:34.030
You know, Chapel Rona really knows what she's doing.
你知道的，查佩尔·罗娜（Chapel Rona）真的很清楚她在做什么。

703
00:26:34.590 --> 00:26:35.950
She knows she can say yes or no.
她知道自己可以拒绝或接受。

704
00:26:35.950 --> 00:26:38.030
If someone holds something up like, what about this?
如果有人拿出一个东西说，这个怎么样？

705
00:26:38.110 --> 00:26:38.750
No.
不。

706
00:26:38.750 --> 00:26:40.190
That's not part of it.
那不是其中一部分。

707
00:26:40.190 --> 00:26:40.670
That's not part of it.
那不是其中一部分。

708
00:26:40.670 --> 00:26:42.190
That's not what we're doing here.
那不是我们在这里做的事。

709
00:26:42.190 --> 00:26:43.070
Yeah, it's true.
是的，确实如此。

710
00:26:43.070 --> 00:26:45.390
I mean, there's inspiration, there's improvisation,
我的意思是，有灵感，有即兴发挥，

711
00:26:45.390 --> 00:26:46.510
there's finding stuff in the moment,
有在当下发现灵感，

712
00:26:46.510 --> 00:26:49.870
but I think more to the point is really good comedians
但我觉得更重要的是，真正优秀的喜剧演员

713
00:26:49.870 --> 00:26:53.710
and really good rock stars and really people who really create something.
和真正优秀的摇滚明星，以及那些真正创造出东西的人。

714
00:26:53.710 --> 00:26:54.670
They know what they're doing.
他们很清楚自己在做什么。

715
00:26:54.670 --> 00:26:56.990
Neil knew what he was doing when he became a cartoon
尼尔变成卡通形象时很清楚自己在做什么

716
00:26:56.990 --> 00:26:58.750
and pirouetted onto that counter.
并且在那个柜台上旋转起舞。

717
00:26:58.750 --> 00:27:00.030
He thought about that shit.
他考虑过那破事儿。

718
00:27:00.030 --> 00:27:01.310
He did not think of it in the moment.
他可不是在当下临时想到的。

719
00:27:01.310 --> 00:27:02.830
He planned that.
那是他计划好的。

720
00:27:02.830 --> 00:27:05.790
You see it more, and we'll talk about this in episode two,
你看得越多，我们在第二集里也会讨论这个，

721
00:27:06.350 --> 00:27:09.630
but when Marshall is afraid to leave the apartment
但当马歇尔害怕离开公寓时

722
00:27:09.630 --> 00:27:11.950
when they first moved to New York City and then he gets excited
他们刚搬到纽约市，然后他变得兴奋起来

723
00:27:11.950 --> 00:27:13.470
and he's walking around, I'm like,
到处走动，我就觉得，

724
00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:16.590
he absolutely is playing Marshall like a muppet.
他绝对是把马歇尔当成布偶在演。

725
00:27:16.590 --> 00:27:17.630
Totally, totally.
完全是，完全是。

726
00:27:17.630 --> 00:27:18.750
It's a hundred percent.
百分之百是这样的。

727
00:27:18.750 --> 00:27:19.870
He goes into Fazibear.
他加入了费兹熊。

728
00:27:20.910 --> 00:27:21.310
Totally.
完全同意。

729
00:27:22.110 --> 00:27:23.550
Which is fitting for Jason Siegel.
这很符合杰森·席格尔的风格。

730
00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:24.270
It's true.
确实如此。

731
00:27:24.270 --> 00:27:26.510
It's like, you know, he's like, this is what I'm doing.
就好像，你知道的，他会觉得，这就是我在做的事。

732
00:27:26.510 --> 00:27:27.870
Yeah, and it's funny.
对，而且这很搞笑。

733
00:27:27.870 --> 00:27:31.550
And I think there is such a connection between music and comedy too.
而且我认为音乐和喜剧之间也存在着这样一种联系。

734
00:27:31.550 --> 00:27:33.470
Like, musicians are constructing something
比如，音乐家在构建某些东西

735
00:27:33.470 --> 00:27:35.150
just like comedians are constructing something.
就像喜剧演员在构建某些东西一样。

736
00:27:35.150 --> 00:27:36.590
And I think that's an astute connection.
我觉得这是一个很深刻的联系。

737
00:27:36.590 --> 00:27:38.270
No, I saw something.
不，我看到过一些内容。

738
00:27:38.270 --> 00:27:39.070
I forget where I saw this.
我忘了是在哪看到的了。

739
00:27:39.070 --> 00:27:40.190
I saw a clip.
我看到过一个片段。

740
00:27:40.190 --> 00:27:41.790
It was something that they were talking about actors.
那是关于他们谈论演员的内容。

741
00:27:42.350 --> 00:27:47.470
And they said, if you saw Yo-Yo Ma play like a gorgeous cello
他们说，如果你看到马友友演奏美妙的大提琴

742
00:27:47.470 --> 00:27:49.950
concerto, you'd never be like,
协奏曲，你绝不会产生这种想法：

743
00:27:49.950 --> 00:27:50.750
give me that cello.
把那个大提琴给我。

744
00:27:50.750 --> 00:27:51.230
I can do that.
我也会。

745
00:27:51.870 --> 00:27:52.110
Right.
没错。

746
00:27:52.750 --> 00:27:55.390
But the thing about acting is,
但表演的奇妙之处在于，

747
00:27:55.390 --> 00:27:58.510
if it's really good, it looks like life.
如果演得足够好，它看起来就像生活本身。

748
00:27:58.510 --> 00:28:00.030
And we're all living life, right?
而我们都在经历生活，对吧？

749
00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:01.150
Like, we're not all playing the cello,
就像，我们不是所有人都会拉大提琴，

750
00:28:01.150 --> 00:28:02.670
but we're all living life.
但我们都在经历生活。

751
00:28:02.670 --> 00:28:03.870
So it looks really easy.
所以这看起来真的很简单。

752
00:28:03.870 --> 00:28:04.350
Yeah.
对。

753
00:28:04.350 --> 00:28:08.350
But what's crazy is, it's really hard to act like life.
但疯狂的是，要演得像生活本身真的很难。

754
00:28:08.350 --> 00:28:13.550
It's really hard to do that thing that I've been doing for a long time.
要做到我一直在做的那种事真的很难。

755
00:28:13.550 --> 00:28:16.110
And one of the things I really try to do is make it look like
而我真正努力做的事情之一，就是让它看起来

756
00:28:16.110 --> 00:28:17.870
I'm not working that hard or there's not that much.
我没那么费力，或者没有那么多刻意的痕迹。

757
00:28:17.870 --> 00:28:19.550
Like, I'm just behaving.
就像我只是在自然地表现。

758
00:28:19.550 --> 00:28:22.110
But I have also have my own code and my, you know,
但我也有一套自己的准则，你知道的，

759
00:28:22.110 --> 00:28:26.110
my own things that I am leaning into.
有我自己的偏好和倾注心力的地方。

760
00:28:26.110 --> 00:28:27.070
I wouldn't call them tricks,
我不会称它们为技巧，

761
00:28:27.070 --> 00:28:29.950
but they're, I'm really thinking about what I'm doing.
但它们确实是……我真的在思考我在做什么。

762
00:28:29.950 --> 00:28:34.190
And I have ways to make things feel very natural.
而且我有办法让事情感觉非常自然。

763
00:28:34.190 --> 00:28:36.830
And that's from years of study.
那是多年研究的结果。

764
00:28:36.830 --> 00:28:38.030
You know, that's not like a,
你知道，那可不是那种……

765
00:28:38.030 --> 00:28:41.550
but I do think that because good acting looks lifelike,
不过我的确认为，因为好的表演看起来很生活化，

766
00:28:41.550 --> 00:28:44.590
people overestimate how easily they could do it.
人们往往会高估自己能有多轻松地做到这一点。

767
00:28:44.590 --> 00:28:45.150
Yeah.
对。

768
00:28:45.150 --> 00:28:45.470
Yeah.
对。

769
00:28:45.470 --> 00:28:46.110
I think that's right.
我想是这样的。

770
00:28:46.110 --> 00:28:48.190
You don't go look at like a Matisse painting and go like,
你不会去看一幅马蒂斯的作品然后说：

771
00:28:48.190 --> 00:28:49.630
I could probably do that later today.
我今天晚些时候大概也能画一个。

772
00:28:49.630 --> 00:28:50.110
Yearly.
每年都这样。

773
00:28:50.110 --> 00:28:52.030
But with acting, it's a little bit,
但对于表演来说，有点不太一样，

774
00:28:52.030 --> 00:28:53.310
but it's frustrating as an actor,
作为演员，这挺令人沮丧的，

775
00:28:53.310 --> 00:28:56.670
because how often you kind of encounter that feeling of like,
因为你经常会遇到那种感觉，就是：

776
00:28:56.670 --> 00:28:58.750
yeah, you just go there and you kind of make the right face
对啊，你只要去到那个现场，然后针对对的时刻

777
00:28:58.750 --> 00:28:59.470
for the right moment.
做出对的表情就行了。

778
00:28:59.470 --> 00:29:00.510
You do your thing.
做你该做的事。

779
00:29:00.510 --> 00:29:03.150
And then, but then if you see someone who's not an actor
然后，但是如果你看到一个不是演员的人

780
00:29:03.150 --> 00:29:04.350
and they got on camera,
站到镜头前，

781
00:29:04.350 --> 00:29:07.230
they really don't know what to do with their arMs.
他们真的不知道该把手放在哪里。

782
00:29:07.230 --> 00:29:08.510
Their face gets weird.
他们的脸会变得很不自然。

783
00:29:08.510 --> 00:29:09.070
Yeah.
对。

784
00:29:09.070 --> 00:29:12.110
Like it really is genuinely weird and off-putting.
这真的非常诡异且让人不舒服。

785
00:29:12.110 --> 00:29:12.670
Yeah.
对。

786
00:29:12.670 --> 00:29:15.070
And you realize it once there's a camera pointing at you,
一旦有镜头对准你，你就会意识到这一点，

787
00:29:15.070 --> 00:29:15.630
like I'm right there.
就像我现在这样。

788
00:29:15.630 --> 00:29:16.590
Like even on this podcast,
就像在这个播客上，

789
00:29:16.590 --> 00:29:17.230
like there's a camera,
有个镜头对着我，

790
00:29:17.230 --> 00:29:19.150
like my stupid computer camera is looking at me.
我那破电脑摄像头正对着我呢。

791
00:29:19.150 --> 00:29:21.230
Like I never feel comfortable being on a camera.
我从来没觉得对着镜头拍有多自在。

792
00:29:21.230 --> 00:29:22.030
Like even right now,
就像现在也是，

793
00:29:22.030 --> 00:29:24.030
and we've done like 80 of these episodes.
我们都录了差不多 80 期这样的节目了。

794
00:29:24.030 --> 00:29:26.910
Like there's something you look ridiculous.
就是会觉得你看起来很滑稽。

795
00:29:26.910 --> 00:29:28.910
You are being so awkward.
你显得太尴尬了。

796
00:29:28.910 --> 00:29:29.630
No, I'm kidding.
不，开玩笑的。

797
00:29:29.630 --> 00:29:32.510
There is, there's something you have to conquer about,
